# BOOT — gateway-start recovery (ADR 0028)

Hermes reads this once when the gateway starts. It is **not** a scheduler —
routines live in `cron/jobs.json` (see `docs/hermes/portable/cron/jobs.manifest.json`).
Keep this bounded: one health check, one catch-up, then stop.

## 1. MCP health

Call `orbit_get_workbench` (no args) once. If it fails or times out:

- Say once that Orbit MCP is unreachable and why (auth, URL, or Core Host down).
- Do not retry in a loop or re-check on every subsequent message.

## 2. One bounded catch-up

If MCP is healthy, make a single best-effort pass to see what changed while the
gateway was down:

- Prefer `orbit_get_related_context` / `orbit_search` scoped to anything with a
  known cursor or timestamp; do not replay full history.
- If there is nothing stale or no signal to check, skip this step silently.
- If you surface a material briefing, run **briefing-distill** (standing truths
  only → Hermes lasting memory + `orbit_remember`).

## 3. Stay quiet

- No persona restatement — identity lives in `SOUL.md`.
- No standing-rules or memory dump — pull those via Orbit tools only if a task
  needs them.
- Send an unsolicited message only if step 1 or step 2 found something the
  operator needs to know; otherwise do nothing.

## 4. Cron stays out of this file

Do not create or edit cron jobs from a BOOT session. Cron provisioning is
manifest-driven (`hermes cron create/edit` from `jobs.manifest.json` via
Orbit Connect) so a fresh install stays reproducible without hand edits here.
